{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on probable outcomes, where an outcome is considered probable if its probability is greater than 1 divided by the number of outcomes. Lottery A has 2 outcomes: 6000 Euros (25%) and 0 Euros (75%). Thus, 1 divided by 2 is 0.5. So, an outcome is probable if its probability > 0.5. Here, 75% > 0.5, so 0 Euros is probable; 25% < 0.5, so 6000 Euros is not probable. Therefore, only the 0 Euro outcome is probable in A. Lottery B has 3 outcomes: 4000 Euros (25%), 2000 Euros (25%), and 0 Euros (50%). 1 divided by 3 is approximately 0.333. So, outcomes with probability > 0.333 are probable. Thus, 50% > 0.333 → 0 Euros is probable; 25% < 0.333 → both 4000 and 2000 are not probable. So, in B, only the 0 Euro outcome is probable. Therefore, both lotteries have exactly one probable outcome (0 Euros), so the average number of probable outcomes is equal (1 each). The reason prefers the lottery with a higher average of probable outcomes — since both have the same average (1), the reason is indifferent. However, the reason is defined to prefer the one with a higher average, so if averages are equal, no preference is established. Thus, the reason can be applied to this decision problem, and the relevant information is the identification of probable outcomes based on probability thresholds.",
